<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TModuleView</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.3.3" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.TModuleView" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TModuleView</tt> renders its child controls only when a named application module is
present and a condition is true. When the module is absent or the condition is false, the
control renders an optional fallback template in place of its children. A page uses it to
show content that depends on a module, such as an extension, without failing when the
module is not installed.
</p>

<h2>Properties</h2>
<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td><tt>ModuleId</tt></td><td>The id of the application module to check for. The control is active when the application has a module with this id.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>Condition</tt></td><td>A PHP expression that is evaluated in the template control. The control is active when the expression is true. The default is <tt>true</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>FallbackTemplate</tt></td><td>A template that is rendered when the control is not active. It is optional.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>IsActive</tt></td><td>Read-only. True when the module is present and the condition is true.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>ModuleAvailable</tt></td><td>Read-only. True when the application has a module with the id in <tt>ModuleId</tt>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><tt>Module</tt></td><td>Read-only. The module instance, or null when the module is absent.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Showing content for a module</h2>
<p class="block-content">
The child controls of a <tt>TModuleView</tt> render when the module named by
<tt>ModuleId</tt> is present. This example shows a link only when the application has a
<tt>payments</tt> module. When the module is absent, the control creates none of its child
controls.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TModuleView ModuleId="payments"&gt;
  &lt;com:THyperLink Text="Manage payments" NavigateUrl="?page=Payments" /&gt;
&lt;/com:TModuleView&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>A fallback template</h2>
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>FallbackTemplate</tt> renders when the control is not active. It is written as a
<tt>prop:FallbackTemplate</tt> tag inside the control. This example shows the module
content when the module is present, and a message when it is absent.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TModuleView ModuleId="payments"&gt;
  &lt;com:THyperLink Text="Manage payments" NavigateUrl="?page=Payments" /&gt;
  &lt;prop:FallbackTemplate&gt;
    Payments are not enabled.
  &lt;/prop:FallbackTemplate&gt;
&lt;/com:TModuleView&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<h2>A condition</h2>
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>Condition</tt> property adds an expression that also has to be true for the
control to be active. The expression is evaluated in the template control, so it reads the
page's properties. This example shows the child controls only when the module is present
and the user is an administrator.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TModuleView ModuleId="payments" Condition="$this->User->isInRole('admin')"&gt;
  &lt;com:THyperLink Text="Refund" NavigateUrl="?page=Refund" /&gt;
&lt;/com:TModuleView&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The control is active only when it has a <tt>ModuleId</tt> and the module is present. A
control with no <tt>ModuleId</tt> is not active, and it renders the fallback template. To
show content on a condition alone, use <a href="?page=Controls.Conditional">TConditional</a>.
</p>

